1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,861
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,861 --> 00:00:07,192
This is chapter twenty-seven: locks and atomics.

3
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:09,903
synchronized is the right default.

4
00:00:09,903 --> 00:00:17,882
But there are four requirements it can't express, and each one has a purpose-built tool in java dot util dot concurrent.

5
00:00:18,782 --> 00:00:21,776
Four requirements synchronized can't express.

6
00:00:21,776 --> 00:00:27,430
Give up after a timeout, because checkout can't block forever while a customer waits.

7
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:33,284
Let readers proceed concurrently, because prices are read constantly and updated rarely.

8
00:00:33,284 --> 00:00:37,941
Wait for something to happen, like all vendor connections being ready.

9
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:42,398
And limit concurrency, say to twenty calls against a warehouse API.

10
00:00:42,848 --> 00:00:46,431
You could build each from synchronized and wait-notify.

11
00:00:46,431 --> 00:00:50,144
The result would be longer, slower, and wrong more often.

12
00:00:51,044 --> 00:00:52,473
The core distinction.

13
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:58,393
A lock protects any block of code, and under contention threads wait, parked by the OS.

14
00:00:58,393 --> 00:01:04,654
An atomic protects one variable, using compare-and-swap, and under contention threads retry.

15
00:01:04,654 --> 00:01:12,208
So atomics can't deadlock and never park, but under heavy contention every thread spins retrying and burns CPU.

16
00:01:12,208 --> 00:01:16,495
Locks are cheaper when sections are long or contention is high.

17
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:22,009
And almost everything here is built on one class, AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.

18
00:01:22,009 --> 00:01:28,257
It's an atomic int that means something different to each tool, plus a queue of parked threads.

19
00:01:28,257 --> 00:01:31,217
Understand that, and you understand them all.

20
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:39,721
ReentrantLock adds what synchronized lacks: timeouts, interruptible waits, fairness, and multiple conditions.

21
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:46,069
Here, tryLock waits at most two hundred milliseconds, then reports busy instead of hanging.

22
00:01:46,519 --> 00:01:50,705
That turns an indefinite hang into a bounded, reportable failure.

23
00:01:50,705 --> 00:01:59,852
It also breaks one of the four deadlock conditions, because a thread that can't acquire gives up instead of waiting while holding what it has.

24
00:02:00,302 --> 00:02:02,765
And the finally is non-negotiable.

25
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:07,475
synchronized releases its monitor on any exit, even an exception.

26
00:02:07,475 --> 00:02:11,387
A ReentrantLock is released only when you call unlock.

27
00:02:11,387 --> 00:02:15,662
Miss it on an exception path, and the lock is held forever.

28
00:02:16,562 --> 00:02:22,465
For read-mostly data, StampedLock offers an optimistic read that takes no lock at all.

29
00:02:22,465 --> 00:02:27,749
Read a stamp, read the data, then validate that no write happened in between.

30
00:02:27,749 --> 00:02:31,593
Only on a conflict do you fall back to a real read lock.

31
00:02:31,593 --> 00:02:36,878
Two cautions: it's not reentrant, so taking it twice on one thread deadlocks.

32
00:02:36,878 --> 00:02:39,623
And validate before using what you read.

33
00:02:40,073 --> 00:02:45,160
But when updates replace the whole value, there's often a simpler answer.

34
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,296
An immutable map behind a volatile reference.

35
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:51,501
Readers take no lock, writers swap atomically.

36
00:02:51,501 --> 00:02:52,895
That beats any lock.

37
00:02:53,795 --> 00:02:54,362
Atomics.

38
00:02:54,362 --> 00:02:57,555
incrementAndGet gives exact sequence numbers.

39
00:02:57,555 --> 00:03:01,670
updateAndGet runs a compare-and-swap loop with a function.

40
00:03:01,670 --> 00:03:06,707
Under contention that function runs more than once, so it must be pure.

41
00:03:06,707 --> 00:03:12,099
No logging, no side effects, or your audit log records things several times.

42
00:03:12,549 --> 00:03:16,389
And for counters many threads hammer, use LongAdder.

43
00:03:16,389 --> 00:03:21,338
With AtomicLong, every thread CASes the same memory and most retry.

44
00:03:21,338 --> 00:03:25,769
LongAdder spreads writes across cells and sums them on read.

45
00:03:25,769 --> 00:03:32,047
So AtomicLong for exact values like IDs, LongAdder for metrics you read occasionally.

46
00:03:32,947 --> 00:03:36,708
For coordination, a CountDownLatch waits for n events.

47
00:03:36,708 --> 00:03:42,906
Each vendor connection counts down, and await blocks until all are ready, with a timeout.

48
00:03:42,906 --> 00:03:49,104
A Semaphore bounds concurrency: acquire a permit, call the warehouse, release in finally.

49
00:03:49,104 --> 00:03:51,123
At most twenty calls at once.

50
00:03:51,573 --> 00:03:54,884
CountDownLatch is one-shot and can't be reset.

51
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:59,347
For threads that meet repeatedly, use CyclicBarrier or Phaser.

52
00:03:59,347 --> 00:04:06,976
And Semaphore pairs naturally with virtual threads: ten thousand can exist while only twenty hold permits.

53
00:04:07,876 --> 00:04:10,502
One subtle trap: the ABA problem.

54
00:04:10,502 --> 00:04:12,015
Thread one reads A.

55
00:04:12,465 --> 00:04:14,363
Thread two changes it to B.

56
00:04:14,813 --> 00:04:16,115
Then back to A.

57
00:04:16,565 --> 00:04:20,928
Thread one's compare-and-swap succeeds, assuming nothing changed.

58
00:04:20,928 --> 00:04:22,069
It changed twice.

59
00:04:22,519 --> 00:04:27,737
CAS only checks that the value is equal, not that it was never modified.

60
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:32,157
It matters mostly in lock-free structures that recycle nodes.

61
00:04:32,157 --> 00:04:37,810
The fix is AtomicStampedReference, which pairs the value with a version stamp.

62
00:04:37,810 --> 00:04:42,231
For immutable values that are never reused, it doesn't arise.

63
00:04:43,131 --> 00:04:44,391
Production rules.

64
00:04:44,391 --> 00:04:46,467
Use synchronized by default.

65
00:04:46,467 --> 00:04:48,320
Always unlock in finally.

66
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,250
Use tryLock with a timeout where a caller is waiting.

67
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:58,106
For read-mostly data, try an immutable value behind a volatile reference first.

68
00:04:58,106 --> 00:05:04,037
Use LongAdder for contended metrics, and Semaphore to respect downstream limits.

69
00:05:04,487 --> 00:05:07,458
Never put side effects inside updateAndGet.

70
00:05:07,458 --> 00:05:10,913
Never re-acquire a StampedLock on the same thread.

71
00:05:10,913 --> 00:05:16,717
Don't make locks fair without measuring, because fairness costs a lot of throughput.

72
00:05:16,717 --> 00:05:18,721
Never reuse a CountDownLatch.

73
00:05:18,721 --> 00:05:24,455
And never build your own synchroniser from wait and notify when one already exists.

74
00:05:25,355 --> 00:05:25,989
To recap.

75
00:05:25,989 --> 00:05:28,031
Locks wait and atomics retry.

76
00:05:28,031 --> 00:05:33,453
ReentrantLock adds timeouts and interruption, but you must unlock in finally.

77
00:05:33,453 --> 00:05:38,875
For read-mostly data, use an optimistic StampedLock, or just a volatile swap.

78
00:05:38,875 --> 00:05:43,099
Use LongAdder for hot counters, and keep CAS functions pure.

79
00:05:43,099 --> 00:05:49,014
And use the built-in latches, barriers and semaphores rather than hand-rolling them.

80
00:05:49,464 --> 00:05:52,024
Next is chapter twenty-eight: executors.

81
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:53,304
Thanks for watching.
